<description>
Reveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.

Input structure:
- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.
- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.
- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.

Rule:
- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.
- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.
- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.

Key insight:
The marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "solid rectangular marker over a mirrored color pattern",
  "dst": "hidden marker-sized rectangle",
  "transform": "replace each marker pixel by the matching pixel from its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart when that orbit is also hidden",
  "grid_shape": "crop to marker bbox"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: NxN square, N=30 [observed]; lo=2 [observed]
  colors: 8 non-marker colors from {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,9}; marker color is always 8
  symmetry: |
    The clean grid (marker replaced by true values) has bilateral LR+UD symmetry:
    - columns [lo..N-1] are LR-symmetric about center (lo+N-1)/2, for ALL rows 0..N-1
    - rows [lo..N-1] are UD-symmetric about center (lo+N-1)/2, for ALL cols 0..N-1
    The lo x lo top-left corner and the outer border rows/cols 0..lo-1 are free
    (not constrained by the bilateral symmetry), but their [lo..N-1] segments
    inherit the same LR/UD mirror axis as the inner block.
  marker:
    shape: solid filled rectangle, color 8
    size: 3-9 rows x 3-9 cols, area 16-36 [observed]
    placement: anywhere in the grid; may touch/overlap edges or outer border
    constraint: the unique color whose bounding box is entirely filled (area > 1)
    recovery: each marker pixel is recovered via its LR/UD reflection orbit; if all
      orbit members are also in the marker (happens when marker overlaps cols/rows < lo),
      use transpose fallback g[c,r]
  output: the marker-sized rectangle of the clean pattern hidden under the marker

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    - Pick N (e.g. 30), lo (e.g. 2). Choose 8 non-marker colors from {0-7,9}.
    - Build the clean symmetric pattern as a quarter tile Q of size (N-lo+1)//2 x (N-lo+1)//2
      filled with random colors, then mirror Q left-right and up-down to fill the
      (N-lo) x (N-lo) inner block at [lo..N-1, lo..N-1].
    - Fill outer border rows 0..lo-1 by randomly coloring their [lo..N-1] segments
      with LR symmetry matching the inner axis, and freely filling the lo x lo corner.
    - Copy outer border cols 0..lo-1 analogously (UD-symmetric on [lo..N-1] segment,
      corner already set).
    - Choose marker rectangle size (h x w, each 3-9, area >= 16). Place it randomly.
      If marker overlaps the outer border (rows or cols < lo), ensure that for every
      marker cell (r,c) whose full reflection orbit stays inside the marker,
      clean[c,r] == clean[r,c] (transpose fallback constraint).
    - Overwrite the marker region with color 8 to produce the input grid.
    - The output is the clean values in the marker rectangle.
```
